tory, 1619-1895: From the Colonial Period to the Age of Frederick Douglass, accessed April 21, 2015, via Oxford African American Studies Center database:b(Whipper, William; social reformer, entrepreneur, abolitionist, journalist; born c.1804 in Lancaster, Pennsylvania, United States; bought holdings in Reading and Columbia Railroad Company; contributed one thousand dollars per year to the Underground Railroad (1847-1860); started the American Moral Reform Society (1835); editor of the National Reformer; member of the American Anti-Slavery Society and National Negro Convention movement; participated in planning the National Council of Colored People (1853); treasurer of  Philadelphia Building and Loan Association and a cashier of Freedman's Bank; died 09 March 1876 in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, United States)  aIndividual was an abolitionist.02457cz  a2200253n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075035002300092040003100115046002300146100003100169370006200200372003200262373017600294374003100470375000900501670007900510670005900589670030800648670121100956678003602167nr 96027777 DLC20150525073622.0960731n| azannaabn          |n aaa     c  anr 96027777   a(OCoLC)oca04135575  aMWAbengerdacMWAdDHU-MS  f18100804g189804151 aPurvis, Robert,d1810-1898  aCharleston (S.C.)bPhiladelphia (Pa.)cUnited States2naf  aAntislavery movements2lcsh  aAmerican Moral Reform SocietyaPennsylvania Anti-Slavery SocietyaVigilant Committee of PhiladelphiaaAmerican Anti-Slavery SocietyaAmerican Negro Historical Society2naf  aCivil rights workers2lcsh  amale  aHis A tribute to the memory of Thomas Shipley, 1836:bt.p. (Robert Purvis)  aLC in RLIN, 7/31/96b(hdg.: Purvis, Robert, 1810-1898)  aDictionary of Amer. Negro bio., 1982b(Purvis, Robert, Sr.; abolitionist and civil rights leader; prominent in the organization of the American Anti-Slavery Society at Phila., 1833; member of the Pennsylvania Society for Promoting the A