1751-17611 aCrichton, Wm.q(William),d1733-1782  aData provided by the ESTC/BL  aCrichton, C. Unto the Right Honourable, the Lords of Council and Session, the petition of Charles, William, and Alexander Crichtons, sons of deceased Patrick Crichton of Newington, late saddler in Edinburgh, 1761:btext (fl. 1751-1761)  aA letter to the Right Honourable the lords commissioners of His Majesty's Treasury, 1777:bp. 27 (signed, Wm. Crichton)  aOxford dictionary of national biography WWW site, 4 Mar., 2020b(Crichton, William (1733-1782), merchant and politician, was the son of Patrick Crichton, a saddler and merchant of Edinburgh; Crichton's main line of business was the West Indies trade but he diversified his activities to include the purchase of property in Edinburgh and London, marine insurance broking, lending on mortgage, and investment in stocks and securities; In September 1780, following the death of John Kirkman, Crichton was elected alderman of Cheap ward and shortly afterwards he became one of the two sheriffs of the City of London;  died as a widower on 30 July 1782 at Chillingham Barns, Northumberland, having become ill during a journey on horseback to London from Edinburgh where his brother, Alexander, lived and worked as a coachmaker)uhttps://doi.org/10.1093/ref:odnb/40651  aThe Peerage WWW site, 4 Mar., 2020b(William Crichton; b. 26 Oct 1733)uhttp://www.thepeerage.com/p37535.htm#i375350  aNotes and records, the Royal Society journal of the history of science, 1984:bvol. 38, no. 2, p. 241 (Tansey, E.M. The life and works of Sir Alexander Crichton, F. R. S. (1763-1856); Sir Alexander's grandfather, Patrick Crichton, was a saddler and ironmonger in the Canongate, Edinburgh; on his death in 1759 the property passed to the two surviving sons of his twenty-two children: William, a London alderman and High Sheriff of Middlesex, and Alexander, a coachmaker in Newington)00870cz  a2200205n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010