z  a2200217n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075035002300092040003400115046001500149100003000164370006200194374002900256375001700285377000800302400002900310670006400339670015600403nr 96039504 DLC20210811064612.0961101n| azannaabn          |a aaa     c  anr 96039504   a(OCoLC)oca04209098  aNICbengerdacNICdDLCdCU-I  f19332edtf1 aNgô, Đình Chương  aQuảng Bình (Vietnam)cVietnameHuế (Vietnam)2naf  aPoetsaTranslators2lcsh  aMales2lcdgt  avie0 aNgô Đình Chương  aMy version of Kieu, 1993:bcover (Ngô Đình Chương)  aHà Huyền Chi. Bên trời mài kiếm, 1998:bt.p. (Ngô Đình Chương) p. 9 (Ngo Dinh Chuong; b. 1933 in Quang BInh; grew up in Hue)01393cz  a2200181n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075035001700092040003600109046000900145100002400154400003200178667003300210670021900243670074900462nr 96039507 DLC20130313142420.0961101n| azannaab           |a aaa     c  anr 96039507   a(Uk)tb710436  aESTC-NAbengerdacESTC-NAdDLC  g16601 aPerry, Joan,d-16601 wnneaaPerry, Joan,dd. 1660  aData provided by the ESTC/BL  aOverbury, T. A true and perfect account of the examination, confession, trial, condemnation, and execution of Joan Perry, and her two sons, John and Richard Perry, for the suppos'd murder of William Harrison, 1736.  aDNB:bv. 14, p. 1276 (Sir Thomas Overbury, the younger (d. 1684) ... wrote in 1676 'A true and perfect account of the examination, confession, trial, condemnation, and execution of Joan Perry and her two sons for the supposed murder of William Harrison, Gent.'. Harrison, who was steward to the Viscountess Campden at Campden, was a neighbour of Overbury, and on 16 Aug. 1660 disappeared mysteriously, whereupon his servant, John Perry, asserted that he, with his mother and brother, had murdered his master. Although John's story was wholly uncorroborated, the three persons incriminated were arrested, tried, convicted and hanged; but subsequently Harrison returned home, stating that he had been kidnapped and been sold as a slave in Turkey)02051cz  a2200217n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010003100075035001700106040003600123046000900159100003600168400003900204400003500243400003500278667003300313670020600346670074900552670053201301nr 96039510 DLC20130712150506.0961101n| azannaab           |a aaa     c  anr 96039510 znr 96022647   a(Uk)tb710439  aESTC-NAbengerdacESTC-NAdDLC  s16601 aHarrison, William,dactive 16601 wnneaaHarrison, William,dfl. 16601 aHarrison, William,cof Campden1 aHarrison, William,cof Cambden  aData provided by the ESTC/BL  aA true and perfect account of the examination, confession, trial, condemnation, and execution of Joan Perry, and her two sons, John and Richard Perry, for the suppos'd murder of William Harrison, 1736.  aDNB:bv. 14, p. 1276 (Sir Thomas Overbury, the younger (d. 1684) ... wrote in 1676 'A true and perfect account of the examination, confession, trial, condemnation, and execution of Joan Perry and her two sons for the supposed murder of William Harrison, Gent.'. Harrison, who was steward to the Viscountess Campden at Campden, was a neighbour of Overbury, and on 16 Aug. 1660 disappeared mysteriously, whereupon his servant, John Perry, asserted that he, with his mother and brother, had murdered his master. Although John's story was wholly uncorroborated, the three persons incriminated were arrested, tried, convicted and hanged; but subsequently Harrison returned home, stating that he had been kidnapped and been sold as a slave in Turkey)  aThe power of vvitchcraft, being a most strange but true relation of the most miraculous and wonderful deliverance of one Mr. William Harrison, of Cambden in the county of Glocester, steward to the Lady Nowel. Who was supposed to have been murthered by his own servant, and his servants mother and brother: but to the amazement of all the people that live near the said place, the truth is now brought to light; and Mr. Harrison after about two years absence is returned into his own country and place of abode in Cambden, 1662.00625cz  a2200157n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075040002600092046001500118100001900133670014900152670011400301675005200415nr 96039523 DLC20211230102925.0961101n| acannaab           |n aaa     c  anr 96039523   aPUbengcPUdDLCerda  f19492edtf1 aGratz, Michael  aVernünfte und Mythen, c1996:bt.p. (Michael Gratz) p. 4 of cover (Literaturwissenschaftler at the Ernst-Moritz-Arndt-Universität Greifswald)  aSchwarz, S. Werke, Briefe, Dokumente, 2021:bv. 1, t.p. (Michael Gratz) p. facing t.p. (b. 1949 in Merseburg)  aKürschners Deutscher Gelehrten-Kalender, 199200763cz  a2200181n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075035002300092040004900115100009200164400008200256400008800338500005000426670004100476670006400517nr 96039529 DLC20160713075356.0961101n| azannaabn          |a aaa     c  anr 96039529   a(OCoLC)oca04200249  aNIC-MubengerdacNIC-MudNBuUdDLCdCtY-Mus1 aGabrieli, Giovanni,dapproximately 1554-1612.tCanzoni et sonate.pSonata,mvoices (15)1 aGabrieli, Giovanni,dapproximately 1554-1612.tSonatas,mvoices (15),nK. C871 wnneaaGabrieli, Giovanni,dca. 1554-1612.tCanzoni et sonate.pSonata,mvoices (15)1 wriDerivative (work):aGordon, Geoffrey.tXIX  aGabrieli, G. Sonata XIX a 15, c1970.  aKentonb(C87, Sonata XIX a 15; no. 19 in Canzoni et sonate)00616nz  a2200145n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075035001700092040002400109100001400133667003300147670029000180nr 96039533 DLC19961106052635.5961101n| acannaab|          |n aaa     c  anr 96039533   a(Uk)tb064836  aUk/ESTC-NAcESTC-NA1 aFirth, J.  aData provided by the ESTC/BL  aHis J. Firth, mercer, woollen and linen draper, haberdasher, and hosier, (late partner with Mr. Wrighton