 October 1964 in Plettenberg, North Rhine-Westphalia) is a German economist and independent investment fund manager. He is a professor for general and international business administration at the University of Applied Sciences Worms and head of the Cologne-based IFVE Institut für Vermögensentwicklung GmbH (IFVE: a privately owned institute for asset management), which he founded in 2003.uhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Max_Otte00641cz  a2200169n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075040001800092100002700110400004200137670005200179670009600231670005400327670009000381nr 96043379 DLC20060207125320.0961204n| acannaab           |a aaa     c  anr 96043379   aNjPcNjPdDLC1 aIsmael, Jenann,d1968-1 aIsmael, J. T.q(Jenann Tareq),d1968-  aEssays on symmetry, 1997:bt.p. (Jenann Ismael)  aInfo. from Princeton University Archives, Oct. 10, 1996b(Ismael, Jenann, b. Jan. 27, 1968)  aThe situated self, 2006:bECIP t.p. (J.T. Ismael)  aInformation from publisher, Feb. 7, 2006b(J.T. Ismael; Jenann Tareq Ismael; b. 1968)02994cz  a2200277n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075035002300092040003700115046001300152100003300165370007700198372016200275373025200437374009300689374003200782374004700814375000900861377000800870670007500878670010200953670006301055670159801118nr 96043386 DLC20150716073822.0961204n| azannaabn          |n aaa     c  anr 96043386   a(OCoLC)oca04223070  aNjPbengerdacNjPdDLCdNNYU-HJ  f196204251 aDoran, Michael Scott,d1962-  aKokomo (Ind.)fCarmel (Ind.)fFullerton (Calif.)eWashington (D.C.)2naf  aNational security--United States--Decision makingaMiddle East--Politics and government--1945-aArab-Israeli conflictaUnited States--Foreign relations2lcsh  aStanford UniversityaPrinceton UniversityaNational Security Council (U.S.)aHudson InstituteaBrookings InstitutionaNew York UniversityaUniversity of Central FloridaaUnited States. Department of StateaUnited States. Department of Defense2naf  aAuthorsaJournalistsaTelevision journalistsaConsultantsaMiddle East specialists2lcsh  aResearch institutes--Fellow  aFaculty member, College or university2dot  amale  aeng  aThe politics of Pan-Arabism, 1997:bvol. 1, t.p. (Michael Scott Doran)  aInfo. from Princeton University Archives, Nov. 15, 1996b(Doran, Michael Scott, b. Apr. 25, 1962)  aPan-Arabism before Nasser, 1999:bCIP t.p. (Michael Doran)  aHudson Institute website, viewed July 9, 2015b(Michael Doran is a Senior Fellow at the Hudson Institute in Washington, DC. He specializes in Middle East security issues. In the administration of President George W. Bush, Doran served in the White House as a senior director in the National Security Council, where he was responsible for helping to devise and coordinate United States strategies on a variety of Middle East issues, including Arab-Israeli relations and U.S. efforts to contain Iran and Syria. He also served in the Bush administration as a senior advisor in the State Department and a deputy assistant secretary of defense in the Pentagon. Born in Kokomo, Indiana, Doran went to elementary school in Carmel, outside of Indianapolis, before his family moved to Fullerton, California, where he graduated from Sunny Hills High School. He received a B.A. from Stanford and an M.A. and Ph.D. in Near Eastern Studies from Princeton. Before coming to Hudson, Doran was a Senior Fellow at the Brookings Institution. He has also held teaching positions at NYU, Princeton, and the University of Central Florida. He is the author of Pan-Arabism before Nasser, which analyzes the first Arab-Israeli war as an inter-Arab conflict, and is now completing a book for Simon and Schuster about President Eisenhower and the Middle East. He appears frequently on television, and has published extensively in Foreign Affairs, The American Interest, Commentary, Mosaic Magazine, The Wall Street Journal, The Washington Post, and The New York Times.)uhttp://www.hudson.org/experts/1035-michael-doran01401cz  a2200253n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075035002300092040003700115046002100152100003500173368002900208373006700237374001800304375000900322377000800331400003500339670006300374670010300437670014900540670045800689nr 96043392 DLC20210305164745.0961204n| azannaabn           a aaa     c  anr 96043392   a(OCoLC)oca04223076  aNjPbengerdacNjPdDLCdNNdDLC  f1968-09-042edtf1 aGlaude, Eddie S.,cJr.,d1968-  cAfrican American authors  aPrinceton University. Center for African American Studies2naf  aAuthors2lcsh  amale  aeng1 wnneaaGlaude, Eddie S.,d1968-  aThe language of nation, 1997:bt.p. (Eddie S. Glaude, Jr.)  aInfo. from Princeton University Archives, Sept. 30, 1996b(Glaude Jr., Eddie S., b. Sept. 4, 1968)  aAfrican American religious thought, c2003:bt.p. (Eddie S. Claude, Jr.) p. 4 of cover (assoc. prof., religion and African studies, Princeton U.)  aPrinceton University, Center for African American Studies, April  18, 2014b(Eddie Glaude, Jr.;  Ph.D., Princeton University;  William S. Tod Professor of Religion and African American Studies, Department of Religion, and Chair, Center for African American Studies. Professor Eddie Glaude's research interests include American pragmatism, specifically the work of John Dewey, and African American religious history and its place in American public life)01840cz  a2200277n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010004500075035002300120040005500143046001500198100002700213370006500240372005800305374002300363377000800386378002200394400003300416670028200449670008100731670010600812670011300918670017201031670035901203nr 96043396 DLC20200128073610.0961204n| azannaabn           a aaa     c  anr 96043396 zno 98083349 zno 98109221   a(OCoLC)oca04223079  aNNFrbengerdacNNFrdDLCdOCoLCdDLCdOCoLCdMoSU  f19492edtf1 aFarr, Carol A.,d1949-  cUnited St