ol of Fine Arts (1953-1954); worked part-time as merchant marine and as animator at Walt Disney Studios (1951-1958); exhibited in galleries, teahouses, and jazz clubs (1950s); set up studio, West Eighty-fifth Street, in New York (1958), later on Jefferson Street (1962), then on Bowery Street (1963-1978); participated in the civil rights movement (1960s); became art director for Black Arts Repertory Theatre and School; participated in many group and traveling exhibitions; taught studio art courses at University of California, Hayward (1970-1973); established Kenkeleba House for exhibitions, performance, dance and literature in New York City (1974); began Summer Arts program at East Second Street (1979-1982); exhibited in the United States, South America, Africa, Taiwan; retrospective exhibition, New Jersey State Museum, Trenton (1996))  aThe world goes pop, 2015:bpage 214 (Joe Overstreet, b. 1933 in Conehatta, Mississippi. Lives and works in New York City)  aNew York times WWW site, viewed June 11, 2019b(in obituary published June 10: Joe Overstreet; b. June 20, 1933, Conehatta, Miss.; the family moved to California in the 1940s, settling in Berkeley; Overstreet moved to New York City in 1958; d. June 4, Manhattan, aged 85; artist and activist who in the 1960s took abstract painting into the sculptural dimension and later created a home in New York for artists who had been ignored by the mainstream)00457nz  a2200133n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075040001700092100002600109400003300135670015500168nr 97003524 DLC19970129053620.9970128n| acannaab|          |a aaa        anr 97003524   aDLC-RcDLC-R1 aYoung, Louise,d1960-1 aYoung, Louise Conrad,d1960-  aJapan's total empire, 1997:bCIP t.p. (Louise Young) data sheet (Louise Conrad Young; b. 4/2/60) inside flaps (asst. prof. of history, New York Univ.)00503nz  a2200181n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075040001300092130004600105642001