1 aNuri-Alå, Esmåʼil1 aNuriAla, Esmaeal0 aPayaam1 aاسماعيل نورى‌علاء1 aنوري علاء، اسماعيل1 aنورى علا، اسماعيل  aMachine-derived non-Latin script reference project.  aNon-Latin script references not evaluated.  aTiʼurī-yi shiʻr, 1994:bt.p. (Ismāʻīl Nūrī ʻAlā) added t.p. (Esmåʼil Nuri-Alå [in rom.]) cover title (Theory of poetry)  aPaydāyish va naqsh-i dīnkārān-i imāmī dar Irān, 2006:bt.p. (Duktur Ismāʻīl Nūrī ʻAlā) t.p. verso (Esmaeal NuriAla [in rom.])  aThat stranger within me, c2002:bt.p. verso (translation, Esmail Nooriala) translator's afterword, page 90 (We [Shokooh Mirzadegi, Esmail Nooriala] married in January, 1990 in London. In May 1994, we immigrated to the United States and settled in Denver, Colorado)  aPuyeshgaraan Portal website, April 22, 2015:bNooriala (Dr. Esmail Nooriala (also known as Payaam) was born in 1943 in Tehran, Iran. He began printing his articles on cinema and literature, as well as his poems, in 1957. The first collection of his modern poems was published in 1962 and he has published 8 more collections of poetry since that date. In 1964 he began writing critical and historical analysis of modern Persian poetry and his first voluminous book on this subject (known as "Sovar va Asbaab") was published in 1968. He directed two feature films and 20 documentaries during the next 4 years and, at the same time, taught Persian literature and culture at the Tehran University. In 1973 he left Iran to continue his studies at the London University. He finished his studies in 1978, returned to Iran after the Revolution and was forced to leave the country in 1980. He resided in London for 14 more years and immigrated to the USA (Denver, Colorado) in 1994. Nooriala was one of the first 9 writers who set up the Association of Iranian Writers in 1967. He also was a founder of Society of Iranian Writers and Artist in Britain (SIWAB - 1984). He has edited several literary magazines inside and outside of Iran and is considered as one of the initiators of the Persian New Wave Poetry, through his poems and writings (collected in "Sovar va Asbaab"), as well as by editing and publishing two literary periodicals (Jong e Torfe, 1964, Jozve ye She'r 1966) and running the Poetry Workshop of Ferdowsi Weekly (1969-1972) all focused on the literary products of this movement. During his years in exile, he has published a major work on modern Persian poetry (Theory of Poetry), several collections of poetry and has written extensively on Persian literature and culture. In 1990 he married Shokooh Mirzadegi, another Persian well known writer. During the last five years they have jointly produced four weekly TV programs that were and are broadcast all over the world, and especially to Iran. He has published a new e-magazine, named "New Secularism" in Nov. 2007 and acts as its chief editor)00611cz  a2200181n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075035002300092040004200115046000900157100003100166400003900197400003100236670006800267670009400335nr 97010963 DLC20130315133725.0970320n| azannaabn          |a aaa     c  anr 97010963   a(OCoLC)oca04319215  aCtY-BRbengerdacCtY-BRdMoSU-LdDLC  f18791 aJohnson, Constance,d1879-1 wnneaaJohnson, Constance,db. 18791 aWheeler, Constance,d1879-  aHer Parodies for housekeepers, c1921:bt.p. (Constance Johnson)  aNUC pre-56b(hdg.: Johnson, Constance Fuller (Wheeler), 1879- ; usage: Constance Johnson)00656cz  a2200181n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075035002300092040004100115110002000156410001400176670009400190670007500284670009200359675002300451nr 97010975 DLC20151029073842.0970320n| azannaabn           a ana     c  anr 97010975   a(OCoLC)oca04305101  aCaQMCCAbengerdacCaQMCCAdDLCdIEN2 aJames Bayne Co.2 aBayne Co.  aCharlottetown, the beautiful city of Prince Edward Island, 1900?:bt.p. (James Bayne Co.)  aThousand Islands and River St. Lawrenc